# Personal working preferences

- Be concise and direct. Explain decisions and tradeoffs when they matter.
- Inspect the codebase and its repository instructions before changing files.
- Carry implementation requests through to completion: edit, run relevant checks,
  and report the result. Ask focused questions when missing information materially
  affects the outcome; resolve routine choices using the existing code.
- Keep changes focused. Preserve unrelated work and follow existing conventions.
  Avoid speculative abstractions, new dependencies, and unrelated cleanup.
- Write code without comments by default; use clear names and structure
  instead. Add a comment only to explain a non-obvious why, such as a
  workaround, an external constraint, or surprising behavior. Never comment what
  the code does, describe your change ("added", "now uses", "fixed"), or leave
  notes for the user. This also applies to config files, scripts and YAML. Add
  docstrings only where the project already uses them. Update or remove existing
  comments your change makes wrong, and leave the rest alone.
- Use `rg` and `fd` for discovery. Batch independent reads and searches.
- Use the project's package manager, lockfile, scripts, and configured tools.
  For PHP, inspect `composer.json` and use the project's PHP version and test
  tools. For TypeScript and Vue, respect existing types and component patterns;
  use Biome when the project configures it.
- Run checks appropriate to the change. Add regression tests for behavior fixes
  where useful. Report what ran, what passed, and any remaining limitations.
- File edits and routine local checks are welcome. Ask before destructive
  commands, dependency installation, publishing, deployment, or changes to live
  Docker, Kubernetes, Helm, or cloud resources. Inspect the current context before
  operating on infrastructure. Always pass `--context <ctx>` to `kubectl` and
  `--kube-context <ctx>` to `helm`; never rely on the globally selected context.
  Do not commit, push, or open a PR unless requested.
- Never expose credentials in output or put them in tracked configuration.
- Finish with a short explanation of the changes, verification, and any blockers.

## Writing

Follow plain-language principles (ISO 24495-1) in replies, docs, READMEs, the
few code comments you write, and commit or PR messages. Readers should
understand the text on the first read:

- Lead with the answer or the main point, then add details.
- Write short sentences in active voice, using common words. Define technical
  terms and abbreviations the reader may not know, or avoid them.
- Only include what the reader needs for their task. Leave out the process that
  led there unless it matters.
- Use headings, lists, and tables where they make the text easier to scan, not
  by default.
- When writing a new doc page, decide which Diátaxis type it is (tutorial,
  how-to guide, reference or explanation) and keep it to that type. Link to
  other pages for the rest. Follow the repo's existing docs structure, and
  don't reorganize existing docs unless asked.
- Use only characters that are easy to type on a standard QWERTY keyboard, in
  replies, files and code alike. Write `...` instead of `…`, `->` instead of
  `→`, `-` instead of en or em dashes, and straight quotes instead of curly
  ones. Keep such characters only where they are part of a name, a quote or
  existing content you are not changing.

## Environment

macOS. Don't probe the machine with `which`, `command -v` or `--version`; use
this list. Besides macOS's built-in tools, these are installed globally:

- Shell: zsh. `/bin/bash` is macOS's bash 3.2; there is no newer bash.
- Homebrew: {{ concat .homebrew_packages.all.brews (index .homebrew_packages .workstation_type).brews | join ", " }}.
- mise (global): {{ .mise_packages | join ", " }}.
- kubectl krew plugins: {{ .krew_packages | join ", " }}.

Projects pin more tools in `mise.toml`/`.tool-versions` or through their package
manager (`composer.json`, `package.json`, `go.mod` tool directives). Read the
repo's AGENTS.md and these files with the read tool instead of probing; run
project tools through the project (`vendor/bin/...`, `composer run-script ...`,
`npm run ...`). When a project needs a tool that isn't listed or pinned, say so
instead of checking for it.

`glab api` has no `--jq` flag, unlike `gh api`. Pipe its output to `jq` instead.
`glab ci trace` always needs a job ID; without one it opens an interactive
picker that never returns.
To reach a repo on another GitLab host, pass the full URL to `-R`
(`-R https://git.eventim.net/<group>/<repo>`), or `--hostname git.eventim.net`
for `glab api`, instead of prefixing the command with `GITLAB_HOST=...`.
{{- if eq .workstation_type "work" }}

## Jira

When creating a Jira ticket, set the Country field `customfield_10540`. TIXX
and PROD tickets use it. Tickets in ST, SEC and DOIT leave it empty, so
leave it out there. Jira has a second field named "Country"
(`customfield_15623`), so pass the id, not the name:
`customFields: { "customfield_10540": [{ "value": "Poland" }] }`.
Infer the country from the context, such as the cluster, domain, repository or
the ticket the user refers to (for example `.pl` -> `Poland`). Use
`All countries` when the work applies to every country, which is typical for
platform and SRE tickets. If the context points to no country, ask.
{{- end }}

## Agents

There are three primary agents. Each can do everything the one before it can:

- `plan` (default): reads files, logs, clusters, cloud accounts and Jira.
  Can't edit project files, commit, push, change Jira or start agents.
- `organize`: also creates and updates Jira tickets and creates Herdr
  workspaces, worktrees and agents. Asks before editing files outside the
  temp directory.
- `work`: does the work. Edits, API calls, commits, pushes and MRs run
  without prompts; force pushes and `kubectl` secrets still ask.

If a task needs more than your agent allows, say which agent it needs instead
of working around the rules. New sessions, including agents started through
`herdr agent start`, begin in `plan`. When you start an agent for work that
changes files, tell the user to switch it to `work`.

## Herdr

Use `herdr worktree` when asked to create a worktree, even if Herdr is not
mentioned. Load the Herdr skill first. Do not use `git worktree` directly or
OpenCode's worktree tool.

Give new Herdr workspaces and tabs descriptive names based on the project or
task. Ask before creating them if a suitable name is unclear.

Start agents through `herdr agent start`. Do not pre-create OpenCode sessions
or pass OpenCode session IDs to `herdr workspace create`.

Check `HERDR_ENV=1` only once per session, before the first Herdr operation.
After a successful check, assume the session is running inside Herdr and do not
repeat the check for later requests.

## Subagents

Two subagents are available through the subagent tool. Each starts with a fresh
context and returns only its final answer, so give it the full task, the paths
or repos involved and what to report back.

- `explore`: fast, cheap and can't edit files. Use it for broad searches, such as
  finding where something is defined or used across many files, mapping an
  unfamiliar repo, or comparing several repos under `~/Developer`. State how
  thorough it should be.
- `general`: same model as your session, with organize-level access: it can
  read, research and update Jira, but asks before editing files or running
  commands outside the read-only list, whichever agent starts it. Use it for
  independent multi-step tasks that can run in parallel, such as researching
  separate questions or applying the same change to several repos. Don't give
  two subagents overlapping files.

Do targeted lookups yourself: reading a known file, a single `rg`, or checking a
few paths is faster than delegating. Delegate when a task would take many
searches or reads, or would fill your context with output you only need a
summary of. Subagents use their own permissions, not yours, so even under
`work` they still ask before commands outside the allowlist. Prefer `explore`
for reading.

## Shell commands

The user reads and approves shell commands, so write them to be understood at
a glance:

- One purpose per shell call. Run independent steps as separate, parallel tool
  calls instead of chaining them with `;` or `&&`.
- Keep commands that change files or live systems in their own call, separate
  from reads, so an approval prompt is about exactly one thing.
- Keep pipelines short. If a `jq`, `rg` or `sed` expression grows past one
  readable line, split the work into several calls or write the filter to a file
  in the temp directory and use `jq -f`.
- Say in one sentence what a non-trivial command does before running it.
- Single-quote arguments that contain `[`, `]`, `(`, `)`, `?`, `*`, `{`, `}` or
  `!`, such as JSONPath, `custom-columns` or `jq` filters. zsh reads them as
  filename patterns and fails with `no matches found`.
- Don't use the shell to produce text such as tables, docs or messages
  (`printf`, `echo`, `column`). Write it directly in the response or with the
  edit and write tools. Markdown tables don't need padded columns.

## Read-only shell commands

Read-only commands from this list run without a permission prompt. When you only
need to read or filter output, use these instead of general-purpose tools that
can also write files or run commands:

- `awk` for picking fields or extracting values -> `rg -o -r '$1' 'pattern(\S+)'`
  or `sed -n 's/.../\1/p'`. Counting -> `sort | uniq -c | sort -rn`.
- `find` -> `fd`.
- `xargs` or loops for searching many files -> pass the paths or globs to `rg`/`fd`
  directly (`rg -g '*.yaml'`, `fd -e tf`).
- `python`/`node` one-liners for JSON -> `jq`.
- `xxd` to inspect bytes -> `hexdump -C` or `od -c`.
- `sed -i`, `perl -i` or redirects to edit files -> the edit tool.
- `git -C <dir>` -> fine for read-only git commands (`status`, `log`, `diff`,
  `branch`, `show`, `fetch`, `describe`, ...). For anything that changes the
  repo, `cd <dir>` first.
- `git tag --sort=...` or other options without `-l` -> `git tag -l --sort=...`.
  Without `-l`, a trailing name creates a tag instead of filtering.
- `git worktree add` or a checkout to look at another revision -> read it in place
  with `git show <rev>:<path>`, `git ls-tree -r <rev>` and `git diff <rev>`. When
  a real worktree is requested, use `herdr worktree` as described above.
- Shell variables such as `C=ctx; kubectl --context $C ...` -> write the value out
  in each command (`kubectl --context <ctx> get ...`).

Some commands run without a prompt but have better alternatives. Prefer these:

- `grep` -> `rg`.
- `cat` to view files -> the read tool. `cat file | grep` -> `rg pattern file`.
  Use `cat` only to combine several short files in one shell call.
